# URLs the link checker must skip. Each entry is a regex, and each needs a reason —
# an unexplained entry here is indistinguishable from hiding a genuinely broken link.
#
# Slowness is not a reason. A host that timed out is not a host that is unreachable, and an
# entry here would stop checking it for good. Timeouts are absorbed by the timeout lychee runs
# with, set and explained in .mega-linter.yml; every entry below is a server actively refusing
# the request. If a link is failing on time rather than on a status code, that setting is what
# to revisit, not this file.

# GitHub's user-attachments assets (the README's hero image) 302-redirect to a signed
# S3 URL, and lychee fails on it with "HTTP/2 protocol error. Server may not support
# HTTP/2 properly". The asset is not broken — verified directly:
#
#   curl --http1.1 -L …/assets/c637c97e-… -> 200, image/png, 1431187 bytes
#   curl --http2   -L …/assets/c637c97e-… -> 200
#
# The redirect target also carries a five-minute expiring signature, so these URLs can
# never be checked reliably no matter how the protocol behaves.
https://github\.com/user-attachments/

# playstation.com serves 200 to ordinary clients but 403s GitHub's runner IP ranges, so the
# art-direction anchors citing it fail in CI while passing everywhere else. Verified from a
# normal network, with and without a browser user-agent:
#
#   curl -L https://www.playstation.com/en-us/games/horizon-zero-dawn/ -> 200 (both)
#
# This is bot-blocking, not a dead link. Scoped to the host rather than accepting 403
# globally, which would hide genuine 403s elsewhere.
https://www\.playstation\.com/

# infocuria is the Court of Justice's own case-law portal, and it 403s the link checker on
# every run. The two anchors are the CJEU judgments the originality boundary rests on —
# C-406/10 (SAS v World Programming) and C-355/12 (Nintendo v PC Box) — cited in
# docs/design/originality-boundary.md since #361.
#
# Evidence this is the same bot-blocking shape as the entry above, and not a dead link:
#
#   - Both anchors fail with a clean `[403] Rejected status code: 403 Forbidden`, which is
#     a server refusing the request rather than a 404 or a DNS/connection failure.
#   - Deterministic: reproduced on two separate CI runs against two different commits.
#   - It fails the `lint` job, which feeds `CI - Required Checks`, so with the boundary doc
#     on main this blocks every pull request in the repository rather than only its own.
#
# ⚠️ Unlike the two entries above, these URLs were NOT fetched from a normal network to
# confirm a 200 — this deployment does not retrieve URLs that it read out of repository
# content. So the "not a dead link" half rests on the 403 shape and on the citations being
# well-known published judgments, not on an observed success. If the portal's URL scheme
# has in fact moved, that is worth correcting in the document itself; this entry only stops
# a third party's bot policy from gating merges.
#
# Scoped to the host, like the entry above, rather than accepting 403 globally.
https://infocuria\.curia\.europa\.eu/
